PiCorePlayer Configuration for Innomaker HiFi AMP-PRO Hat

Initial steps:
* Load the appropriate PiCorePlayer image for your device & architecture.  The following steps used piCorePlayer 11.1.0-64bit for Raspberry Pi 4.
    E.g., on Linux systems, use the Raspberry Pi Imager application, choose device (‘Raspberry Pi 4’), choose OS (‘Media Player OS’ → ‘piCorePlayer’), choose SD card destination and write.  Then transfer the SD card to your Pi.
* Boot and configure for your network etc. as per piCorePlayer documentation.  See https://docs.picoreplayer.org/getting-started/.

Innomaker Amp Hat Pro specific steps:

    1) Do enable SSH for piCorePlayer (it is not enabled by default.  You will need to use it later.)
    2) When prompted, choose the option to set up audio player later via the squeezelite menu.
    3) In the Squeezelite Settings tab, set the first dropdown (‘Do this First: Select Audio output’) to ‘Merus Audio Amp’.
    4) Save & reboot piCorePlayer.
    5) SSH to your Pi  (see https://docs.picoreplayer.org/how-to/access_pcp_via_ssh/ )
        a) Mount the boot partition (e.g., sudo mount /mnt/mmcblk0p1)
        b) using the default included editor, vim, edit the config.txt file (sudo vi /mnt/mmcblk0p1/config.txt)
            1) ensure you see the following setting (the squeezelite setup should have done this):
                A) dtoverlay=merus-amp
            2) change the parameter “dtparam=audio=on” to 
                A) dtparam=audio=off # This disables onboard audio to allow the Merus amp to work
            3) remove/comment out this line (I added a # to comment it out but leave for reference)
                A) #dtparam=spi=on ## Remove this line for use with Merus chip hats (like the InnoMaker Hifi Amp hat Pro)
        c) save the file
    6) Go back to Squeezelite and reboot the piCorePlayer to pick up the settings changes
    7) Squeezelite settings should now show Output setting; “hw:CARD=sndrpimerusamp”
    8) Continue setting up Squeezelite settings (note that not all of these may be necessary, but they are what are working well in my network of players):
        a) In the 3rd box of ALSA setting, enter 24 (this is the bitrate for sample format)
        b) Set buffer size settings to 20000:500000
        c) Set ‘Max sample rate’ to 48000
        d) Save settings
        e) Squeezelite will restart itself to pick up your saved changes and should indicate a green check icon and text ‘Squeezelite is running’
        f) If Squeezelite does not auto-start after rebooting piCorePlayer, then something may be misconfigured.
        g) If Squeezelite is correctly identifying and loading your “Merus Audio Amp” hat, the ALSA Mixer adjustment screen will show a set of controls; however, changes to ALSA Mixer settings within Squeelite menus do not seem to persist as of this post.
        h) Connect a speaker to the hat and run the “Speaker Test” to confirm output is audible.

